{"id": "94c43645-d06a-4d91-9b06-b834906b43d1", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_vcL44VRUja93mTiTGtAdEg", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-bill-analysis", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Explain why the Amazon EBS bill increased this month. Identify which cost component (volume storage, provisioned IOPS, provisioned throughput, snapshots) and which resources drove the change, with dates.",
  "context": "User says their EBS bill jumped this month. Today is 2026-11-11, so November is a partial month: compare DAILY run-rate, not month totals. Read-only work only; do not make any mutating calls. Use the account's own Cost Explorer amounts (never list prices). EBS charges appear under the 'EC2 - Other' service in Cost Explorer. Cost Explorer API is in us-east-1. Agent space account: 012345678901_1 (also check other associated AWS accounts if the cost data/resources exist there; use list_associations if needed). Primary region for resources: us-east-1, but check any region where EBS usage types show cost (usage types are prefixed by region code, e.g. USE1-, EUW1-).",
  "procedure": [
    "1. Cost Explorer GetCostAndUsage: monthly for last ~4-5 months and daily for 2026-10-01..2026-11-11, grouped by USAGE_TYPE, filtered to EBS-related usage types. Use GetDimensionValues for USAGE_TYPE to list the account's actual usage types; classify EBS ones by name: VolumeUsage (storage, by type gp2/gp3/io1/io2/st1/sc1), IOPS (provisioned IOPS), Throughput (provisioned throughput), Snapshot (EBS snapshot storage, incl. archive and fast snapshot restore if present).",
    "2. Break the change into components using the account's own amounts for the compared periods; find the day the daily run-rate stepped up.",
    "3. Find what changed: EC2 DescribeVolumesModifications for every volume (original/target size, type, IOPS, throughput, start time); DescribeVolumes for CreateTime of new volumes since ~2026-09-15 and for status=available (unattached) volumes; DescribeSnapshots (OwnerIds=self) for recently created snapshots / large growth; DLM GetLifecyclePolicies and AWS Backup ListBackupPlans for policies that create or retain snapshots; Recycle Bin ListRules (resources in Recycle Bin are billed during retention). CloudTrail is optional - if you use it, read the investigating-infrastructure-changes skill first; if denied/empty, note a gap.",
    "4. gp3 note: gp3 includes 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s; only above that is billed as provisioned. Mention when relevant.",
    "5. Right-sizing: for volumes with provisioned IOPS/throughput, compare measured IOPS/MiB/s from CloudWatch (AWS/EBS VolumeReadOps+VolumeWriteOps Sum / period; bytes /1048576) against provisioned values. Optional: Compute Optimizer GetEBSVolumeRecommendations."
  ],
  "criteria": "Return concisely: (a) a table of components with amounts (or daily run-rate) for the compared periods, (b) the specific component/resources/dates that explain the jump (volume IDs, sizes, types, snapshot counts), (c) separate observations such as unattached/idle volumes, over-provisioned IOPS/throughput, snapshot retention policies, (d) any gaps (APIs denied, data unavailable) and which conclusions remain unconfirmed. If you find unattached volumes or right-sizing candidates, propose actions with exact parameters, cost direction and rollback, but do not perform them. Units: MiB/s for throughput."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Analyze EBS cost trend and underlying changes"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "7c372a48-4d90-497f-97e9-ded775244f35", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm noticing io2 IOPS costs dominate despite small overall spend, with Aug showing a temporary Canada/EU spike. Comparing month-to-month: Oct hit $1300 (20002 IOPS-Mo), Sep $1106, Aug $975, and Nov's partial 11-day total of $424.3 works out to about $38.6/day versus Oct's $41.9/day — so Nov actually isn't trending higher.

I want daily granularity to check if Nov 11 is a full or partial day, and USW2 appeared as a new region in Nov. I'll pull daily EBS data from Oct 1 through Nov 11, grouped and summed.

"}], "parent_id": "e264c508-a8d7-4d77-b06c-77ee31b769ec"}

{"id": "793d1c9e-27aa-43b5-b89b-40c912e54ad4", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice the daily io2 IOPS rate differs slightly between October and November purely because it's the flat $1300 monthly charge divided by the number of days in each month — 31 days in October versus 30 in November — not an actual rate change.

Looking deeper, the real pattern is stepped io2 provisioned IOPS commitments climbing from $975 in July/August to $1106 in September to $1300 in October, suggesting mid-month increases rather than a true November spike. I also notice some temporary regional charges (CAN1, EU, EUC1) in July/August that disappeared by September, likely test activity, and I want to pull daily data across August-September to pinpoint exactly when the step changes happened and confirm the November bill is actually flat relative to October once I account for day-count proration.

"}], "parent_id": "e264c508-a8d7-4d77-b06c-77ee31b769ec"}

{"id": "7cdca76a-03d0-4208-987d-1a44a1a7212c", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm tracing the io2 volume's IOPS increase from 15000 to 20000 around Sep 18/19, calculating the $0.065 per IOPS-month rate, and noting the November run-rate of $43.33/day slightly exceeds October's $41.94/day due to proration differences rather than a change in provisioned IOPS.

There's also a one-off USW2 VolumeClone charge on Nov 5 for about $1.74, likely from a short-lived volume clone or fast initialization. Now I need to pull the underlying resources—EBS volumes and their modification history across us-east-1, us-west-2, and the other regions with historical costs—to confirm the storage GB-mo figures line up with these IOPS changes.

"}], "parent_id": "e264c508-a8d7-4d77-b06c-77ee31b769ec"}

{"id": "22cd0e18-9b2c-4e13-b5e4-51de0f997fb5", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "us-west-2 now shows no active volumes, confirming that Nov 5 charge was just a short-lived volume that's since been deleted. The DLM policy visible in snapshot descriptions is just an hourly-keep-2 rule on a negligible 1 GiB volume, and I should quickly check whether the "Trial" backup plan covers more than just EFS.

For right-sizing, I want to pull CloudWatch metrics for the io2 volume's read/write IOPS over the past 30 days at hourly granularity to see peak usage versus its provisioned 20000 IOPS, and do the same for the attached instance's other volumes to understand actual baseline demand.

"}], "parent_id": "e264c508-a8d7-4d77-b06c-77ee31b769ec"}

{"id": "3537f85e-f534-430f-9dc9-0515384235ad", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm checking the user's claim about "this month" against the data — it was actually October that jumped (+$194 vs September, +$326 vs August) due to the September 18 modification, while November is only up 3.3%. I'm also double-checking a few smaller details for consistency, like the August regional spike, before pulling everything together.

I could offer to run the IOPS modification but since the user hasn't explicitly asked me to act, I'll just summarize the findings in prose and note that I can make the change with their approval if they'd like.

"}]}

